 "productionNotes": "The Enzo's production total is genuinely contested, and the disagreement runs through the most authoritative sources rather than the fringe. Ferrari's own model page states that 'only 399 were developed for road use'. That figure is echoed by DK Engineering ('Ferrari states that only 399 (+1) Enzos were made between 2002 and 2004, all of which were completed in left-hand drive'), by classic-car-auctions.info (399 built in 2003 and 2004, with a 400th and final car made for Pope John Paul II) and by RM Sotheby's own 2015 catalogue for the papal car, chassis 141920, which it described as 'the 400th and final Enzo built'. Evo and supercars.net both print 400; supercars.net adds that the run was originally set at 349, extended by 50 to meet demand, then closed with the single Vatican car. Against that, RM Sotheby's 2026 catalogues for the Paris, Arizona and Monterey sales state that 498 Enzos were built in total, of which 111 were specified for the US market, and Wikipedia's current text says 'A total of 498 units were built' while still describing the Vatican car as the 400th. Supercar Nostalgia hedges with 'thought to be around the 500 mark'. None of the sources consulted explains the gap between 400 and 498, and the same auction house has printed both figures in different years, so no total is asserted here. What the sources do agree on: production ran from late 2002 to 2004, every car was built left-hand drive, three development mules (M1, M2, M3) were made on 348-based bodywork, and the 400th car was a 2005-titled example gifted to, and then sold on behalf of, the Vatican. The track-only FXX, the Maserati MC12 and the one-off P4/5 all used Enzo mechanicals and are counted separately by every source that mentions them.",
